<article class="glass-card rounded-2xl p-6 sm:p-8 space-y-8 text-slate-300">
  <div>
    <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-extrabold text-white tracking-tight mb-3">
      How to Calculate Your Target Freelance Hourly Billing Rate
    </h2>
    <p class="text-sm sm:text-base text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
      Transitioning from full-time employment to freelancing requires rethinking how you charge for your time. Unlike salaried employees, freelancers must cover their own health insurance, taxes, software licenses, unbillable administrative hours, and retirement savings. Setting a sustainable hourly rate is critical for long-term business survival.
    </p>
  </div>

  {/* Formula Box */}
  <div class="p-6 rounded-2xl bg-slate-950 border border-slate-800 space-y-3">
    <h3 class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-amber-400">
      Freelance Hourly Rate Formula
    </h3>
    <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-950/40 border border-amber-500/30 text-center font-mono text-sm sm:text-base text-white font-bold">
      Hourly Rate = (Target Take-Home Salary + Annual Overhead Expenses + Taxes) / Annual Billable Hours
    </div>
    <p class="text-xs text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">
      Annual billable hours are typically only 60-65% of total working hours (around 1,000 to 1,200 hours per year), because time spent marketing, invoicing, client communication, and administrative work cannot be billed directly.
    </p>
  </div>

  {/* Worked Numerical Example */}
  <div class="space-y-4">
    <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white">
      Worked Numerical Example: Solo Designer Target Calculation
    </h3>
    <p class="text-sm text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
      Suppose a freelance brand designer wants a target net annual income of $80,000:
    </p>

    <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-slate-950 border border-slate-800 space-y-2 text-xs font-mono">
      <div class="flex justify-between text-slate-400"><span>1. Desired Personal Salary:</span><span class="text-slate-200">$80,000 / year</span></div>
      <div class="flex justify-between text-slate-400"><span>2. Business Overhead (Software, Hardware, Health Care):</span><span class="text-slate-200">$12,000 / year</span></div>
      <div class="flex justify-between text-slate-400"><span>3. Self-Employment & Income Taxes (approx 25%):</span><span class="text-slate-200">$23,000 / year</span></div>
      <div class="flex justify-between text-white font-bold border-t border-slate-800 pt-1.5"><span>Total Required Gross Revenue:</span><span class="text-amber-400">$115,000 / year</span></div>
    </div>

    <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-500/30 space-y-1.5 text-xs">
      <p class="text-slate-300">Assuming 1,100 billable hours per year (25 hours per week for 44 working weeks):</p>
      <div class="text-base font-extrabold text-white font-mono">
        Target Rate = $115,000 / 1,100 hours = <span class="text-amber-400">$104.55 / hour</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  {/* Practical Freelancer Advice */}
  <div class="space-y-3">
    <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white">
      Proven Strategies for Raising Your Hourly Rates
    </h3>
    <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 text-sm text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
      <li><strong>Transition to Value-Based Project Pricing:</strong> Use your target hourly rate internally to estimate project effort, but quote clients fixed project prices tied to business ROI.</li>
      <li><strong>Factor Non-Billable Time Realistically:</strong> Do not assume a 40-hour billable work week. Allow 10-15 hours per week for business development, learning, and admin.</li>
      <li><strong>Review Rates Annually:</strong> Increase your billing rate by 5% to 10% each year to account for inflation, expanding expertise, and increased software overhead.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  {/* Frequently Asked Questions */}
  <div class="space-y-4 pt-4 border-t border-slate-800">
    <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white">
      Frequently Asked Questions About Freelance Rates
    </h3>
    <div class="space-y-4 text-sm">
      <div class="border-b border-slate-800/80 pb-3">
        <h4 class="font-bold text-white mb-1">How many billable hours can a full-time freelancer realistically log per year?</h4>
        <p class="text-slate-400">Most experienced freelancers average 1,000 to 1,200 billable hours per year (approx 20–25 hours per week). The remaining time is spent on administrative tasks, client onboarding, and marketing.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="border-b border-slate-800/80 pb-3">
        <h4 class="font-bold text-white mb-1">Why is a freelancer's hourly rate higher than an employee's salary hourly equivalent?</h4>
        <p class="text-slate-400">Freelancers do not receive paid vacation, health insurance, 401(k) matching, or paid sick leave from an employer. They must also pay self-employment tax and business overhead costs themselves.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="border-b border-slate-800/80 pb-3">
        <h4 class="font-bold text-white mb-1">Should I publish my hourly rate publicly on my website?</h4>
        <p class="text-slate-400">Publishing a baseline rate helps filter out budget-constrained leads, but quoting custom project pricing gives you flexibility to price based on client value and scope complexity.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="border-b border-slate-800/80 pb-3">
        <h4 class="font-bold text-white mb-1">What percentage should I set aside for taxes?</h4>
        <p class="text-slate-400">Freelancers in the US should generally reserve 25% to 30% of net earnings for federal income tax, state income tax, and self-employment tax (FICA).</p>
      </div>

      <div class="pb-1">
        <h4 class="font-bold text-white mb-1">Is this calculation stored anywhere?</h4>
        <p class="text-slate-400">No. All income goals, expense inputs, and calculated rates remain 100% private inside your web browser.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</article>
